title: Behind the curtains
story: fernhill
continues: npcs
description: The study Mrs Kettle was guarding — the hiding spot, the trunk,
  and the diary page that changes what the photograph shows.

---

> examine the long curtains
[OK: contains "deep enough to swallow a person whole"]

# `hiding-spot with position behind`.
> hide behind the curtains
[OK: contains "You slip behind the long curtains"]

# FINDING: hiding changes nothing the player can observe. The room description
# is identical from behind the curtains, the contents list is unchanged, and no
# line marks the player as concealed. Whatever `hiding-spot` protects against,
# nothing in Fernhill exercises it — there is no NPC who enters the study.
> look
[OK: contains "Verity's study, exactly as she left it"]
[OK: contains "You can see a travelling trunk"]

> come out
[OK: contains "You come out of hiding"]

# `a container with max items 3`, holding two.
> open the travelling trunk
[OK: contains "You open the travelling trunk"]
[OK: contains "Inside the travelling trunk you see diary page, half-burned letter"]

# Implicit take: reading something in an open container picks it up first and
# says so in parentheses.
> read the diary page
[OK: contains "(first taking the diary page)"]
[OK: contains "What matters is safe where the fire went, and only the little silver thing will open it"]

# The red herring, and the reason `ask tobias about the bank` exists.
> read the half-burned letter
[OK: contains "(first taking the half-burned letter)"]
[OK: contains "where the deed may rest secure until"]

# `define phrase mantel-hint while the diary page is read` — the room's own
# description grows a clause now that the diary has been read.
#
# RENDERING NIT, asserted as it behaves: the source reads
# `a wide mantel{mantel-hint}, and a tall case clock`, and the interpolation
# lands after the comma, so the output says "mantel, — and over it", with a
# comma-dash collision the author did not write.
> east
[OK: contains "a dead fireplace under a wide mantel, — and over it Verity's photograph"]

# `phrase detail while the diary page is read` — the same object, examined
# before and after the diary, says different things. Compare arrival.transcript,
# where nothing extra is available.
> examine the framed photograph
[OK: contains "Verity at thirty, on the folly steps"]
[OK: contains "on her knee a small steel box with a bright slot in its lid"]

> examine the mantel
[OK: contains "dusted but bare except for a single framed photograph"]
[OK: contains "On the mantel you see a framed photograph"]
